  "final_text": "The forecast is usable, but not as the final number. I'd cook about **740–750 jars** if the canteen will collect the leftovers on Sunday 4 January, and about **710–720** if it won't.\n\n## Can you trust the forecast?\n\n**The 94.2% is correct.** I recalculated it from the CSV and got 94.19%, using the tool's own definition and weeks. It just tells you less than it sounds like:\n\n- **Simply repeating last week's sales scores 92.6%.** The ERP forecast is only 1.6 points better than that, so 94% isn't as strong as it looks on its own.\n- **It leaves out the two sold-out weeks.** Those were weeks when the forecast was well above sales. Including them lowers the figure only slightly (93.9%). But sold-out weeks only show what you had in stock, not what shops wanted, so true demand in those weeks is unknown.\n- **The forecast runs high.** In 2025 it was on average about 15 jars per week above actual sales.\n- **It was slow to react when the organic market chain delisted the soup in April.** It overshot by around 80 jars a week for several weeks afterwards. A yearly percentage hides problems like this.\n- **A typical week is off by about ±35 jars,** in both directions. That spread matters more for planning than the percentage does.\n- **The holiday week was overshot last year.** For the week of 30 Dec 2024 it forecast 703 and sales were 678, down from 746 the week before.\n- **Minor:** the week of 2024-09-16 appears twice in the CSV. It falls outside the 2025 window, so it doesn't affect the 94.2%.\n\n## How many jars for the week of 29 December\n\nThe forecast is **729**. How far above it to cook depends on what is cheaper, running out or having jars left over:\n\n| | Missing jar | Leftover jar | Best level of cover |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Canteen collects leftovers | €1.50 | €0.40 | cover demand about 79% of the time |\n| No canteen | €1.50 | €1.60 | about 48% |\n\nI applied the past year's forecast errors to 729 and calculated the expected cost for each quantity:\n\n- **With the canteen:** the cheapest range is **740–750** (about €19 expected cost per week).\n- **Without the canteen:** it is **710–720** (about €46).\n\nThe cost is fairly flat around these ranges, so ±10 jars makes little difference. Going far outside them does.\n\n**Before you decide, check two things:**\n\n1. **Will the canteen collect on Sunday 4 January?** That's the weekend after New Year, so it may be closed. This changes the answer by about 30 jars.\n2. **The holiday dip may be bigger than the forecast assumes.** Last year sales fell 9% in this week. Applying the same drop to this December's sales gives about 690, while the forecast barely drops (729 against 733 last week). If you think the dip will repeat, go to the lower end: about 720–730 with the canteen, 700 without.\n\n**What I'd do:** call the canteen today. If they're collecting, cook **740**. If not, cook **710**.\n\nShortage costs here only count the €1.50 lost margin. If an empty shelf is worth more to you, the shortage cost goes up and so do these quantities. As the costs file asks, write down your reason if you add that.",
