      "final_text": "Cook **about 710 jars** for the week of 29 December.\n\n**Why it's lower than before:** without the canteen, a leftover jar now costs the full €1.60. A missing jar costs €1.50. Since the two costs are almost equal, the best plan is to aim at the middle of what you expect to sell, about 48% cover, rather than cooking extra as a buffer. The forecast tends to run high, by about 15 jars a week on average, so the best quantity is a bit below its 729.\n\n**Expected cost per week** (the forecast of 729 adjusted by the past year's forecast errors):\n\n| Jars cooked | Expected cost |\n|---|---|\n| 700 | €49 |\n| **710** | **€46** |\n| 720 | €46 |\n| 730 | €50 |\n| 750 | €65 |\n\nAnything from 710 to 720 costs about the same. Above 730 the cost rises quickly, because every extra jar is now a full €1.60 loss.\n\n**If you expect a holiday dip:** last year sales in this week fell 9%. If that happens again, demand would be around 690, and the best quantity drops to about **690–700**. If you think the dip will repeat, use 700. Otherwise, 710.\n\nTwo related points:\n- **This is now the normal situation.** Without the canteen, it's best to cook close to the expected sales every week. The ERP forecast runs high, so cooking exactly what it says will usually leave too many jars over.\n- **Update costs.md** so the €0.40 leftover cost isn't used again by mistake.",
